// #1746 (RC lane): a program that defines its OWN top-level `fn sleep` must
// compile to a VALID module that calls that function -- not the host import.
//
// The RC raw-ABI shim (compile_call.vibe, the #705/#930/#1460 group) rewrites
// a call for the HOST IMPORT's ABI: it untags the Int argument and pushes the
// `Unit` the void import does not return. It dispatched on the NAME alone, so
// a user's `sleep` took the shim too -- `call <guest fn>` followed by that
// extra `i64.const 0`, i.e. a module that FAILED VALIDATION with no diagnostic
// at all. Only VIBE_RC=1 was affected, which is why no gate saw it.
//
// The fix keys on the resolved function INDEX instead: user functions live at
// `fi + func_offset`, so an index below `func_offset` is an import or a
// generated builtin body. The return count does NOT work -- it comes from the
// registry row's `CtUnit`, so the import reports 1 exactly as a guest
// `-> Unit` function does.
//
// The value is the pin, not just compilation: 6 * 7 = 42 can only come from
// this function's own body, so a call routed to the host `sleep` (which
// returns nothing) fails it.
fn sleep(ms: Int) -> Int {
  let mut i = 0
  while i < ms {
    i = i + 1
  }
  i * 7
}

fn main() -> Int {
  sleep(6)
}
